Z||000000||pollock||04-24-2000||09:26 AM||||I've got an 86 with the puny 10.3 gallon tank.  87-88s have a bigger tank right?  What is their capacity?<P>Anybody done this swap?  I believe it's illegal for a salvage yard to sell tanks, but i know one or two "special" yards....<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||lennyb4||04-24-2000||09:31 AM||||Im not sure about 88's but my 87 has the same size on it. I dont think (could be wrong) that GM used a different tank size on any model year.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-24-2000||09:33 AM||||The larger tank is 11.3 gal. and only on 1988 models. I don't know if the swap is a plug and play. I was thinking about doing it also. From what I've seen of the mounts it looks the same but I don't know what the physical dimensions are of the larger tank or if the mounting holes and different.<P>OOPS, There are corrections, 87-88 is 11.9 gals. See post below.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||04-24-2000||09:39 AM||||Not flaming, but what would be the point of adding only 1 extra gallon?  It doesn't seem like it would be worth the effort, although I'm not the one doing it.  Maybe you could add a fuel cell in the front if you really need to add some miles between fill ups.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pollock||04-24-2000||09:43 AM||||You're right, monkey, it's probably not worth it for 1 gal.  Unless you had one laying around for free.  <P>I was thinking that tank was more like 12 gal.... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||sdm1964||04-24-2000||09:55 AM||||I beleive the 87-88 is a 12 gallon tank, and it would be plug and play. There was a post about this along time ago. At 20 miles per gal rough guess, 2 more gallons would be 40 or more miles on the highway. I would be willing to do that mod too. If i am wrong let me know||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||04-24-2000||10:09 AM||||Was running on a highway with no gas stations  and was down to fumes. Finally found a gas station and put 10.8 gal in my 88||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||04-24-2000||10:37 AM||||I have an 88 Formula. With the car sputtering while stoping (gas running to front away from pump) haven't been able to fill in more than 8.75 gallons. Have heard similar from other posted topics before. Ranges from 8.x to 10.x from different years. Unless the tank is free I wouldn't bother to do the change. In that case keep the old one. It may be better. At least my tank has a lot of plastic baffles inside. Don't know exactly the purpose but at least I know they hide my other 2 to 3 gallons. Wish I could find them...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick's Dad||04-24-2000||10:54 AM||||I, too, have a Formula, and, when I was out there sputtering, I seem to remember just over 11 gallons going in (At probably $1.45/gal for prememium, too!)||||reg||1
Z||000009||pollock||04-24-2000||12:08 PM||||An 88 owner could settle it right here-- just look in the owner's manual under capacities.  <P>Should probably be in the Haynes or Chiltons manual too, just don't have one handy....||||reg||4
Z||000010||Oreif||04-24-2000||12:14 PM||||In the "Fiero" book by HPBooks it lists fuel capacity for 84-86 as 10.2 gals. It lists 87-88 as 11.9 gals. I was incorrect before saying it was 11.3. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||04-24-2000||01:27 PM||||Ed Parks said that the larger tank will not fit in the earlier cars.  It is wider and won't fully slip in the tunnel.||||reg||1
Z||000012||sid1200||04-24-2000||01:46 PM||||ok everyone, I've actually fully run out of gas in my 88GT, as in car stopped after sputtering for about 5mins (yes I was in the middle of nowhere and the "fumes" lasted quite a while, 3 miles or so.<P>so the total I could put in was about 11.35 gallons. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||04-24-2000||04:24 PM||||Say: "good-bye" to your fuel pump! The only lubrication the pump gets if from the fuel itself. When you run it dry, you damage your pump (speaking from experience).<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000014||chris6878||04-25-2000||12:39 PM||||yup.. i did that on my 84 4 banger...    had to replace pump.. running them dry is a very bad ideal..        actually.. i wouldnt reccomend going under 1/4 of a tank if at all possible. When going around curves or up and down hills the gas is moving around in the tank and when its low it may move from the pump and not lubricate it like it should...<P>just a thought  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000015||pollock||04-25-2000||01:22 PM||||Running out of gas isn't a very good idea period, so far as i can tell.  I have done it though, and  it didn't kill the pump...<P>Hey, let's keep this thread going.... maybe i'll have my first "on fire" post, hehe....<P>Heard any good jokes lately?<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000016||Capt Fiero||04-25-2000||01:35 PM||||The bigger tanks will not fit into a car that came with the smaller tanks, sorry.  As for running out of gas yes that is bad, Not only will your pump be hurt, but it clogs both of the fuel filters very rapidly. (yes you have 2 filters) Your second filter is in the tank commonly called a sump screen.  They do not need to be changed regularly as they have has gas sloshing around them.  They are connected to the inlet on the pump. Just a side note, It takes something less than the size of sewing thread into your fuel pump to damage it.  The sump screen will stop almost everything, so when changing pumps make sure that it is on and solid.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Dan||04-25-2000||09:21 PM||||Baffles? Baffles are to prevent all the fuel going to one side of the tank and loosing fuel suction-pump cavitation.I have an 86 and just returned from the mountains and found a 5% uphill grade will produce cavitation making the engine misswith 2 gallons still in it.At refill it took 8.3 gallons.Here in Indiana,I can run the tank down to nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Old Lar||04-25-2000||09:52 PM||||Isn't there a fuel pump tube that sucks up the gas?  I've understood that if the filler tube isn't aligned properly, it doesn't draw fuel from the bottom of the tank, so you run out before the tank is really empty.  Thats why you could be out of gas and only have room for 10 gallons in a 11.9 gallon tank.<P>You all know how much fun it is to work on (replace) fuel pumps.  Drop the tank but don't spill the gas.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Standard||04-26-2000||01:36 AM||||I've run out of gas on both my cars.. with the 84, it was my fault.. pumps still working fine after over a year, tho.  with my GT it was because the gas gauge don't work, and I wasn't quite getting as good fuel milage as I thought I was.. pumps still working good, tho.  Knock on SMC  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
